Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Fast Gates on a Transmon", "Title",
 CellID->509267359,ExpressionUUID->"22a1a762-10e3-861e-525e-3f4ca097ed64"],

Cell["\<\
A transmon is a superconducting circuit that behaves as a weakly anharmonic \
oscillator. A qubit is stored in its two lowest levels, and a microwave pulse \
at the qubit frequency rotates it between them. The anharmonicity is small, a \
few hundred megahertz against a qubit frequency of several gigahertz, so the \
transition from |1\:27e9 to |2\:27e9 lies close to the qubit transition. A \
short pulse has a broad spectrum and drives both, carrying population out of \
the qubit. A long pulse avoids that but is exposed for longer to energy decay \
and dephasing.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->329621944,ExpressionUUID->"4da796ef-9f57-a4bf-7b5c-0fc91ae30e85"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "How long the best pulse is, and how much the derivative correction known as \
DRAG shortens it, has no closed-form answer. Both follow from the Schr\
\[ODoubleDot]dinger and Lindblad equations solved with the levels the qubit \
leaks into kept explicitly. For an anharmonicity of \[Minus]200 MHz and \
coherence times ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["T", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1b39f900-6440-cee7-4ac6-f16a0dfa94cd"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["T", "TI"], "2"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "75d93363-5da6-51a4-23e6-ab4d5453238e"],
 " of 100 \[Mu]s, the best \[Pi] pulse below lasts 17.7 ns with an error of \
9.8 \[Times] 10\:207b\:2075 when it uses DRAG, against 2.0 \[Times] 10\:207b\
\:2074 near 32 ns without it."
}], "Text",
 CellID->515379898,ExpressionUUID->"25a568a6-87a2-527f-535a-810032572145"],

Cell["Five Levels of a Transmon", "Section",
 CellID->604760901,ExpressionUUID->"00d7f70d-8d0e-ae34-30ec-ef5724b8f51e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In the frame rotating at the qubit frequency, the transmon is the \
anharmonic oscillator ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["H", "TI"], "0"], "=", 
   FractionBox[
    StyleBox["\[Alpha]", "TI"], "2"], 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["b", "TI"], "\[Dagger]"], 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["b", "TI"], "\[Dagger]"], 
   StyleBox["b", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["b", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2f3e8453-9762-68ea-56c5-1e1d22173a30"],
 ", where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["b", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5902d484-617f-9570-0f31-cdee100bfc5a"],
 " lowers the excitation number by one and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\[Alpha]", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "56e0e12c-de7d-6135-7e9f-f889db08301f"],
 " is the anharmonicity. Five levels are kept here; the last section shows \
that fewer change the answer and more do not."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1476799103,ExpressionUUID->"5708d032-2150-04f2-7d37-ca7c50e56ed8"],

Cell["The lowering operator of a five-level oscillator:", "Text",
 CellID->448336599,ExpressionUUID->"3b7807e8-9504-b25a-6907-9eb21cb600a0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

